WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.188
Aggregate functions take a set of rows and turn that
set into

00:00:03.212 --> 00:00:07.325
one summary value The five functions on this slide
are among the

00:00:07.338 --> 00:00:12.275
most common COUNT answers questions about how many
rows or values exist

00:00:13.162 --> 00:00:18.712
SUM adds numeric values together AVG calculates the
arithmetic mean

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.612
MIN returns the smallest value and MAX returns the
largest value One

00:00:24.638 --> 00:00:27.875
important point is that GROUP BY is not required
every time you

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.850
use an aggregate If you run an aggregate over an
entire table

00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:35.750
without GROUP BY PostgreSQL can return one summary
row for the whole

00:00:35.775 --> 00:00:41.775
table For example SELECT COUNT * FROM sales returns
one number representing

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.688
the number of sale rows GROUP BY becomes useful when
you want

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:51.188
several summaries one for each group For example
total revenue by category

00:00:51.212 --> 00:00:55.762
creates one result row per category Also remember
that the type of

00:00:55.788 --> 00:01:00.588
measure should match the question COUNTing rows is
different from SUMming quantity

00:01:01.462 --> 00:01:07.000
AVG price answers a different question from total
revenue SQL will calculate

00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:10.475
exactly what you request even if the measure is not
meaningful for

00:01:10.488 --> 00:01:15.350
the decision question So before choosing a function
ask what quantity am

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:16.450
I trying to describe
